<h2>Multi-source readings (one measurement read by two sources; the declared owner keeps it)</h2>
{{ mark('multi_source') }}
<p class="note">Declared ownership (D98 / D99, <code>ownership.MULTI_SOURCE_OWNERSHIP</code>): inside a declared scope (model, object label, eval category minus any eval ids the declaration excludes by name, transfer kind; admitted at the served operating point or as unfitted) the two sources' rows are matched by identity (pairing key, eval, metric, threshold), the pairing key being the declaration's pairing rule &mdash; the producer run key both sources spell alike, so an owner row folds only onto the secondary row read from the same store, or empty when the identity alone pairs. Both sides count only their source's own computation rows: a stats reader's blank-computation twin filed onto a scoped run is never in the scope. <strong>folded</strong>: k and n agree exactly, the owner row stands, the secondary row leaves the paper frame and contributes only the declared carry readings where the owner has none. <strong>stopgap</strong>: the owner carries no row yet, the secondary row stands and says so. <strong>refused</strong>: k / n differ (or a side carries two rows, or the owner already carries a different carry reading) &mdash; neither value is admitted, the owner row keeps its identity with value / k / n NULL and this reason, and nothing resolves it by priority. <code>at_cap_share</code> is shown for both sides and never travels (two statistics under one paper name). Refused: <strong>{{ multi_source_counts.refused }}</strong>, stopgap {{ multi_source_counts.stopgap }}, folded {{ multi_source_counts.folded }}.</p>
